Carafe Lab: counter reads, traps and host signals in natively run guest code. Eden's patcher stopped on a read
of CNTFRQ_EL0, which Carafe's runtime makes once; it now gets the console's 19.2 MHz from a trampoline like the
other registers. FEX reads CNTPCT_EL0 in the code it generates, which Linux does not let user code read; the
counter runs at the console's 19.2 MHz on the lab PC, so the trapped instruction is rewritten into a read of
CNTVCT_EL0 and runs on. Any other undefined instruction and every brk go to the process's exception handler as
under the JIT; the runtime continues contexts through one. The cores' threads block the signals sent to the whole
process, such as the lab's SIGUSR1 close, so their handlers never run on a guest stack with guest registers.
GPL-3.0-or-later, as the files it changes.

